## Root cause

The warning is caused by **stale USB-device pointers in the Type-C partner object**. UCSI later tries to remove a sysfs link which has already disappeared when the associated USB device was unregistered.

The chain in this tree is:

```text
ucsi_connector_change()
  -> schedule_work(&con->work)
  -> ucsi_handle_connector_change()
  -> ucsi_unregister_partner()
  -> typec_unregister_partner()
  -> typec_partner_unlink_device()
  -> sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")
  -> kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()
```

### 1. Connector-change work

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1371-1382`

```c
void ucsi_connector_change(struct ucsi *ucsi, u8 num)
{
        struct ucsi_connector *con = &ucsi->connector[num - 1];

        ...
        if (!test_and_set_bit(EVENT_PENDING, &ucsi->flags))
                schedule_work(&con->work);
}
```

The work function is installed in `ucsi_register_port()` at
`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1632`:

```c
INIT_WORK(&con->work, ucsi_handle_connector_change);
```

### 2. Disconnect handling

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1264-1364`

The relevant branch is at approximately lines 1310-1326:

```c
if (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) {
        ...
        if (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {
                ucsi_register_partner(con);
                ...
        } else {
                ucsi_unregister_partner(con);
        }
}
```

### 3. UCSI unregisters the partner and its children

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138-1154`

```c
static void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)
{
        if (!con->partner)
                return;

        typec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);
        ...
        ucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);
        ucsi_unregister_cable(con);
        typec_unregister_partner(con->partner);
        memset(&con->partner_identity, 0, sizeof(con->partner_identity));
        con->partner = NULL;
}
```

The alternate-mode removal is not the source of the `"typec"` warning:

- `ucsi_unregister_altmodes()` — `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:639-673`
- `typec_unregister_altmode()` — `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:707-714`

Those unregister Type-C altmode devices. The `"typec"` link is removed by the USB-device link handling below.

### 4. The offending Type-C class function

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:906-913`

```c
static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner,
                                        struct device *dev)
{
        sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
        sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");

        if (partner->deattach)
                partner->deattach(partner, dev);
}
```

It is called from `typec_unregister_partner()` at
`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:1174-1195`:

```c
if (port->usb2_dev) {
        typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);
        port->usb2_dev = NULL;
}
if (port->usb3_dev) {
        typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);
        port->usb3_dev = NULL;
}
```

The corresponding links are created by `typec_partner_link_device()` at
`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:888-904`:

```c
ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
```

Thus, the exact offending function in `drivers/usb/typec` is:

```text
typec_partner_unlink_device()
drivers/usb/typec/class.c:906-913
```

The UCSI-side function that reaches it is:

```text
ucsi_unregister_partner()
drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138-1154
```

called from:

```text
ucsi_handle_connector_change()
drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1264-1364
```

## Why kernfs reports `ENOENT`

The warning is emitted by:

`fs/kernfs/dir.c:1724-1752`

```c
if (!parent) {
        WARN(1, KERN_WARNING
             "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n", name);
        return -ENOENT;
}
...
kn = kernfs_find_ns(parent, name, ns);
...
